WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.961
This is the executive
briefing for Book 02 of the

3
00:00:03.961 --> 00:00:07.799
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Identity and Directory.

4
00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.933
It's for the people who
make the calls: the CIO,

5
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.763
the CISO, the identity
program lead and the sponsors.

6
00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:16.187
The argument is short.

7
00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:19.668
Zero Trust moved the access
decision onto identity,

8
00:00:19.668 --> 00:00:24.038
so whatever the directory believes,
every application enforces.

9
00:00:24.038 --> 00:00:29.313
Active Directory isn't being retired,
but every road now points to Entra ID,.

10
00:00:29.313 --> 00:00:31.770
And syncing the forest as it stands just

11
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.674
copies twenty-five years
of risk into the cloud.

12
00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:37.912
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

13
00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:42.288
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

14
00:00:42.288 --> 00:00:45.350
Let's start with where
access gets decided now.

15
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.750
For most of the directory era, the
network made the first access decision.

16
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.975
If you were inside, you reached
what the perimeter allowed.

17
00:00:53.975 --> 00:00:57.475
Federal policy moved that
decision onto identity.

18
00:00:57.475 --> 00:01:03.441
M-22-09 says log into applications, not
networks, with phishing-resistant MFA.

19
00:01:03.441 --> 00:01:08.600
NIST's Zero Trust architecture gives no
trust for simply being on the network,

20
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.825
and CISA puts identity
first among its pillars.

21
00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:14.752
So the identity provider
decides every request,

22
00:01:14.752 --> 00:01:17.387
from whatever the
directory says about you.

23
00:01:17.387 --> 00:01:21.362
That's why a wrong attribute is
no longer a typo in a phone book.

24
00:01:21.362 --> 00:01:24.099
It's the wrong access,
enforced everywhere.

25
00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:28.512
And a stale account becomes a valid
identity, usable from anywhere.

26
00:01:28.512 --> 00:01:31.210
So, is Active Directory going away?

27
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.823
Here's the common misconception.

28
00:01:33.823 --> 00:01:38.723
Microsoft isn't retiring Active Directory;
it still supports and improves it.

29
00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:40.559
The pressure comes from direction.

30
00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:43.172
Cloud Sync is replacing Connect Sync.

31
00:01:43.172 --> 00:01:46.772
Microsoft wants federation
moved to cloud authentication.

32
00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:52.385
NTLM is deprecated, and its first version
is already gone from current Windows.

33
00:01:52.385 --> 00:01:57.410
And since January, synced users can be
converted to cloud-managed, one at a time.

34
00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:58.885
Some dates are fixed.

35
00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:04.547
The October 2026 Windows update can
break older Wi-Fi and VPN sign-in.

36
00:02:04.547 --> 00:02:08.171
And by April 7, 2027,
every Connect Sync server

37
00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:12.186
has to be upgraded and
reconfigured, or sync stops.

38
00:02:12.186 --> 00:02:16.341
So the first decision: the sponsor
and CIO put those clocks on the plan,

39
00:02:16.341 --> 00:02:18.747
and set scope and sequence against them.

40
00:02:18.747 --> 00:02:21.120
Next, the trap to avoid.

41
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.721
The tempting plan is to
install a sync engine,

42
00:02:24.721 --> 00:02:27.495
copy the forest as it
stands, and call it done.

43
00:02:27.495 --> 00:02:30.232
The sync works; what it
carries is the problem.

44
00:02:30.232 --> 00:02:34.051
Twenty-five years of old
organization charts, layered policy,

45
00:02:34.051 --> 00:02:38.320
forgotten service accounts and
ownerless trusts land in the tenant.

46
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.320
Delegation has nowhere to go.

47
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.201
A synced admin account lets an
on-premises breach reach the cloud,

48
00:02:44.201 --> 00:02:48.370
which is why Microsoft wants privileged
roles on cloud-only accounts.

49
00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.094
The worst outcome is paying
for a cloud platform

50
00:02:51.094 --> 00:02:53.545
and running it as a copy
of the old directory.

51
00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:58.545
So the decision for the sponsor and
CIO: treat this as a governance event.

52
00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:02.295
Discover, rebuild explicitly,
and prove it survived.

53
00:03:02.295 --> 00:03:05.030
Here's why that rebuild is structural.

54
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.793
In Active Directory, where an object
sits decides how it's governed.

55
00:03:09.793 --> 00:03:12.660
Move a user to another
organizational unit (OU),

56
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.942
and their administrators and policies
change without anyone touching the account.

57
00:03:17.942 --> 00:03:22.444
Entra ID has no tree; it governs
by what an object carries:

58
00:03:22.444 --> 00:03:25.281
attributes, memberships and roles.

59
00:03:25.281 --> 00:03:29.130
So every placement-based rule
has to be rewritten explicitly.

60
00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:33.879
Administrative units (AUs) help, but
they can't nest, and for synced users

61
00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:38.955
the attributes are still written
on-premises, so every change waits on sync.

62
00:03:38.955 --> 00:03:43.478
That makes one decision come early:
which attributes are authoritative,

63
00:03:43.478 --> 00:03:46.267
what they mean, and where
each one is mastered.

64
00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:50.706
HR decides the meaning, and identity
engineering decides the flow.

65
00:03:50.706 --> 00:03:53.810
Now, the part Conditional
Access can't see.

66
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.652
Conditional Access is the
cloud's policy engine,

67
00:03:57.652 --> 00:04:00.435
and it runs when someone
signs in to Entra ID.

68
00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:03.991
A Kerberos ticket or an NTLM
exchange is settled between

69
00:04:03.991 --> 00:04:07.472
a computer and a domain
controller, and never gets there.

70
00:04:07.472 --> 00:04:11.605
That means every remaining
Kerberos or NTLM dependency

71
00:04:11.605 --> 00:04:15.473
is an access path with no
cloud policy decision at all.

72
00:04:15.473 --> 00:04:20.797
And those protocols are on a clock:
NTLM is deprecated, and Microsoft plans

73
00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:25.947
to switch network NTLM off by default
in the next major Windows release.

74
00:04:25.947 --> 00:04:28.760
Wait for cutover, and you'll
find them in production.

75
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.285
So the decision: start NTLM
auditing in discovery.

76
00:04:33.285 --> 00:04:35.595
Application owners move each path,

77
00:04:35.595 --> 00:04:40.535
and anything that stays is an exception
the authorizing official accepts.

78
00:04:40.535 --> 00:04:42.590
And the surface is wider.

79
00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.315
Most migration plans
count users and groups.

80
00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:51.880
But Active Directory has been quietly
holding the rest of the estate together: name

81
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.365
resolution, certificates, network sign-in,
application logins, time and file paths.

82
00:04:57.365 --> 00:05:00.526
The program counts eleven
kinds of dependent object,

83
00:05:00.526 --> 00:05:02.753
and the worst of them don't fail loudly.

84
00:05:02.753 --> 00:05:07.190
Certificates stop renewing weeks
after the migration is declared done.

85
00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:10.765
Wi-Fi and VPN sign-in
fail at the network edge.

86
00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:14.978
Applications bound to a domain
controller just stop letting people in.

87
00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:16.716
Each looks like a local outage.

88
00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:22.190
That's why three adapters are critical:
certificates, network access and LDAP.

89
00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:25.448
The decision for the sponsor:
nothing in Active Directory

90
00:05:25.448 --> 00:05:28.502
is decommissioned until
those three pass validation.

91
00:05:28.502 --> 00:05:30.730
Then, who can manage whom.

92
00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.067
Delegation used to come
free with the tree.

93
00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:37.568
Without it, agencies fall
into one of two traps.

94
00:05:37.568 --> 00:05:40.104
They over-delegate,
handing out tenant-wide

95
00:05:40.104 --> 00:05:42.781
admin roles because
nothing narrower exists.

96
00:05:42.781 --> 00:05:44.177
Or they under-delegate,

97
00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:47.855
and every password reset becomes
a ticket in a central queue.

98
00:05:47.855 --> 00:05:50.825
Administrative units fix
that, in three tiers:

99
00:05:50.825 --> 00:05:55.748
tenant-wide readers for security and
compliance, department units for full

100
00:05:55.748 --> 00:06:00.593
administration, and narrow units for
the help desk, driven by HR attributes.

101
00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:01.756
Two cautions.

102
00:06:01.756 --> 00:06:04.761
The groups that hold admin
roles can't be dynamic,

103
00:06:04.761 --> 00:06:07.843
so they're assigned and
made eligible just in time.

104
00:06:07.843 --> 00:06:12.442
And locking a unit down also blocks
lifecycle and review features,

105
00:06:12.442 --> 00:06:15.118
so that's chosen unit by unit, default no.

106
00:06:15.118 --> 00:06:17.693
Identity engineering decides the model.

107
00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:20.090
Next, who decides the rest.

108
00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:25.752
In Active Directory, one small team ran
the domain and made most of the calls.

109
00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:28.019
Once identity is the control plane,

110
00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:32.941
those decisions belong to more people,
and some carry legal accountability.

111
00:06:32.941 --> 00:06:37.252
Federal policy already asks for
an agency-wide governance body.

112
00:06:37.252 --> 00:06:41.796
The book adds a simple rule: every
decision has exactly one decider,

113
00:06:41.796 --> 00:06:44.027
even when many people do the work.

114
00:06:44.027 --> 00:06:46.983
That matters because the old
habit was letting whoever

115
00:06:46.983 --> 00:06:49.616
could make a change also
decide whether to make it.

116
00:06:49.616 --> 00:06:52.703
That's how an engineer ends
up setting the agency's

117
00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:55.415
risk posture by leaving
legacy sign-in open.

118
00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:58.477
Only the authorizing official
can accept that risk.

119
00:06:58.477 --> 00:07:02.065
So the sponsor and CIO adopt
this matrix at the start.

120
00:07:02.065 --> 00:07:03.670
Here's what it says.

121
00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.995
Here's the matrix, one decider per row.

122
00:07:06.995 --> 00:07:10.389
The sponsor and CIO own
scope, funding, sequence,

123
00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:13.695
and when Active Directory
components retire.

124
00:07:13.695 --> 00:07:18.945
Identity engineering owns the technical
design and each wave's go or no-go.

125
00:07:18.945 --> 00:07:23.333
The CISO sets the security requirements
and the privileged-access model.

126
00:07:23.333 --> 00:07:25.495
HR owns what worker data means.

127
00:07:25.495 --> 00:07:28.945
Application owners decide
for their own applications.

128
00:07:28.945 --> 00:07:32.157
And the authorizing official
accepts residual risk.

129
00:07:32.157 --> 00:07:35.147
Then each team gets only
the role its work needs,

130
00:07:35.147 --> 00:07:39.208
with admin roles held by a small
group and activated just in time.

131
00:07:39.208 --> 00:07:41.120
One row deserves emphasis.

132
00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.033
Cutting off legacy sign-in belongs
to the authorizing official,

133
00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:48.208
because keeping it open is
itself a risk acceptance.

134
00:07:48.208 --> 00:07:53.510
Next, a question every sponsor asks:
doesn't Microsoft already do this?

135
00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.235
Doesn't Microsoft already do this?

136
00:07:56.235 --> 00:08:00.113
Microsoft's tools do their
individual jobs well: sync,

137
00:08:00.113 --> 00:08:04.872
attack detection, policy analysis,
tenant baselines, governance.

138
00:08:04.872 --> 00:08:07.532
But each one keeps its
results in its own portal,

139
00:08:07.532 --> 00:08:09.647
and none of them connects the picture.

140
00:08:09.647 --> 00:08:13.743
No native tool inventories the
OU tree and its delegation,

141
00:08:13.743 --> 00:08:18.435
the trusts, DNS, the full Group
Policy estate or certificate health.

142
00:08:18.435 --> 00:08:23.623
And none keeps one record of the old estate,
the new one and the drift between them.

143
00:08:23.623 --> 00:08:28.398
So the program uses what Microsoft does
well and builds only that missing layer.

144
00:08:28.398 --> 00:08:30.610
Every tool already has an owner.

145
00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.847
The gaps between them don't,
unless someone names one.

146
00:08:33.847 --> 00:08:38.686
The CISO owns evidence and drift,
so the CISO names that owner.

147
00:08:38.686 --> 00:08:40.330
Now, how to begin.

148
00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:45.880
An assessment team rarely gets privileged
access on day one, and it doesn't need to.

149
00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:50.021
An ordinary domain account can
read most of what the plan needs:

150
00:08:50.021 --> 00:08:55.118
the forest's shape, the OU tree, groups,
policy links and certificate templates.

151
00:08:55.118 --> 00:08:57.361
Read-only discovery changes nothing,

152
00:08:57.361 --> 00:09:00.980
and it applies least privilege
to the assessors themselves.

153
00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:06.028
Whatever it can't see becomes a short
written request: read only, specific,

154
00:09:06.028 --> 00:09:10.655
time-bound, for one named account,
and an export wherever one will do.

155
00:09:10.655 --> 00:09:13.153
Never a blanket request for Domain Admin.

156
00:09:13.153 --> 00:09:17.635
Tell security operations first,
because this looks like reconnaissance,

157
00:09:17.635 --> 00:09:20.868
and protect the output, because
it's a map of the estate.

158
00:09:20.868 --> 00:09:25.679
The decision: start now, and elevate
only through that narrow request.

159
00:09:25.679 --> 00:09:27.480
Then build the target.

160
00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.980
Building the target comes
down to three choices.

161
00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:35.862
First, the sync engine: Cloud Sync by
default, Connect Sync only where a

162
00:09:35.862 --> 00:09:41.343
forest genuinely needs it, and any
Connect server upgraded before April 2027.

163
00:09:41.343 --> 00:09:44.748
Second, sign-in: keep password
hash sync on everywhere,

164
00:09:44.748 --> 00:09:49.160
because it's the fallback when other
methods fail, and Microsoft says

165
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.417
agencies that had it recovered from
ransomware in hours, not weeks.

166
00:09:53.417 --> 00:09:56.306
Federation servers become
a retirement target.

167
00:09:56.306 --> 00:10:00.318
Third, and this one is the
CISO's: privileged access.

168
00:10:00.318 --> 00:10:02.593
No one holds an admin role permanently.

169
00:10:02.593 --> 00:10:06.226
Roles are activated just in
time, on cloud-only accounts,

170
00:10:06.226 --> 00:10:11.518
with fewer than five Global Administrators
and two tested break-glass accounts.

171
00:10:11.518 --> 00:10:14.192
The CISO sets that model
before the rollout.

172
00:10:14.192 --> 00:10:16.460
Next, how people sign in.

173
00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.085
Sign-in is where users feel the migration.

174
00:10:20.085 --> 00:10:25.553
M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant
MFA, and its targets were due

175
00:10:25.553 --> 00:10:29.910
at the end of fiscal 2024, so this
is a requirement now, not a goal.

176
00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.472
It names PIV as the simplest route.

177
00:10:32.472 --> 00:10:36.534
That flips the old roadmap,
which saved smart cards for last.

178
00:10:36.534 --> 00:10:39.101
Your workforce already carries the cards,

179
00:10:39.101 --> 00:10:41.960
so certificate-based
sign-in is the fastest win.

180
00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:46.035
Administrators go first,
on security keys or PIV.

181
00:10:46.035 --> 00:10:51.048
Then card holders, managed Windows
devices, and everyone else on passkeys,

182
00:10:51.048 --> 00:10:54.335
in parallel, in roughly
twelve to eighteen weeks.

183
00:10:54.335 --> 00:10:57.560
Text messages and voice
calls go away for staff.

184
00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:01.085
The CISO sets that requirement,
administrators first.

185
00:11:01.085 --> 00:11:03.370
So how does the whole migration run?

186
00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:09.368
The migration runs as eleven phases in
five waves: foundation, then objects,

187
00:11:09.368 --> 00:11:14.870
then privilege and policy, then
authentication, then steady-state governance.

188
00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:16.453
Each phase has a rollback,

189
00:11:16.453 --> 00:11:19.920
and the next one doesn't start
until its criteria are met.

190
00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.605
The program estimates forty-three
to sixty-eight weeks in total,

191
00:11:23.605 --> 00:11:26.595
and those are planning
estimates, not measurements.

192
00:11:26.595 --> 00:11:28.707
What leadership watches are the gates.

193
00:11:28.707 --> 00:11:33.182
Policies aren't enforced until
registration passes ninety-five percent.

194
00:11:33.182 --> 00:11:37.446
Help desk volume has to return to
normal within a week of each wave.

195
00:11:37.446 --> 00:11:41.384
And no policy aimed at everyone
ever goes straight from off to on,

196
00:11:41.384 --> 00:11:44.432
because one mistake can
lock out the whole agency.

197
00:11:44.432 --> 00:11:47.932
Identity engineering approves
each wave on its gate.

198
00:11:47.932 --> 00:11:51.150
And the last step is
retiring Active Directory.

199
00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.669
The sync engine is both
the bridge to Entra ID

200
00:11:54.669 --> 00:11:57.738
and the tether that keeps
Active Directory alive.

201
00:11:57.738 --> 00:12:01.700
As long as it runs, Active
Directory is still in production.

202
00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:05.200
So it goes last, and only
when six things are all true.

203
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.725
Every user comes from HR
or the designated source.

204
00:12:08.725 --> 00:12:11.150
Every group lives in Entra ID.

205
00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:14.250
No application uses on-premises sign-in.

206
00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.663
Every device is managed in the cloud.

207
00:12:16.663 --> 00:12:19.287
Every service account is a cloud identity.

208
00:12:19.287 --> 00:12:25.501
And certificates, DNS, network access and
LDAP applications are moved and validated.

209
00:12:25.501 --> 00:12:29.441
The good news is that authority
now moves one object at a time,

210
00:12:29.441 --> 00:12:31.175
so this happens gradually.

211
00:12:31.175 --> 00:12:34.863
The sponsor retires each
component only after its gate.

212
00:12:34.863 --> 00:12:37.030
Finally, proving it worked.

213
00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:41.980
Everything ends in evidence, and the
question is when it gets produced.

214
00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.099
In the book's reference deployment,

215
00:12:44.099 --> 00:12:47.605
it's produced as the work
happens, not assembled afterward.

216
00:12:47.605 --> 00:12:51.767
Each phase ends in a signed
readiness bundle, and those bundles,

217
00:12:51.767 --> 00:12:56.730
with every drift finding and its fix,
build the evidence as the work happens.

218
00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:00.452
It goes to the agency's own
assessor and authorizing official,

219
00:13:00.452 --> 00:13:04.392
and it lines up with the federal
logging and cloud baseline rules.

220
00:13:04.392 --> 00:13:08.400
Most important, it belongs to the
agency, so the next authorization,

221
00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:12.918
and the next engagement, start from the
current record instead of from zero.

222
00:13:12.918 --> 00:13:17.093
The CISO owns that evidence,
and the ISSO assembles it.

223
00:13:17.093 --> 00:13:19.330
Let's bring the decisions together.

224
00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.180
Here are the decisions in one place.

225
00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.899
The sponsor and CIO go first:
plan against the dates,

226
00:13:25.899 --> 00:13:30.680
treat this as a governance event,
and adopt the one-decider matrix.

227
00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:34.605
HR settles what the attributes
mean before anything is mapped.

228
00:13:34.605 --> 00:13:38.105
Application owners chase down
their old sign-in paths.

229
00:13:38.105 --> 00:13:42.167
Identity engineering approves the
delegation model and each wave.

230
00:13:42.167 --> 00:13:45.579
The CISO names the gap owner,
sets privileged access

231
00:13:45.579 --> 00:13:48.992
and phishing-resistant sign-in,
and owns the evidence.

232
00:13:48.992 --> 00:13:52.155
The authorizing official
keeps the legacy cutoff.

233
00:13:52.155 --> 00:13:57.017
And the sponsor retires Active Directory
one gate at a time, sync engine last.

234
00:13:57.017 --> 00:13:59.267
None of this is a directory upgrade.

235
00:13:59.267 --> 00:14:01.889
Discover what Active Directory encodes,

236
00:14:01.889 --> 00:14:04.742
rebuild it explicitly,
and prove it survived.

237
00:14:04.742 --> 00:14:06.854
Book 02 has the detail.

238
00:14:06.854 --> 00:14:07.670
Thank you.
